{% from "macros.njk" import callout, snippet with context %}

{% set design %}
<section class="docs-section">
  <h2 id="d-usage">Usage</h2>
  <p>Tabs switch between <strong>peer views of the same page</strong> — a
     note's overview, activity and settings. The bar stays put, the content
     below it changes, and exactly one panel is visible at a time.</p>
  <p>Three things in Flint look like tabs. Pick by what the selected state
     means:</p>
  <table class="docs-table">
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Pattern</th><th>Selected state means</th><th>Use for</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td><code>.fl-tabs</code></td>
        <td>“this is the view I'm looking at”</td>
        <td>Peer content inside one page (this component)</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><a href="bottom-nav.html"><code>.fl-tab-bar</code></a></td>
        <td>“this is where I am in the app”</td>
        <td>Top-level destinations pinned to the shell</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><a href="chips.html"><code>.fl-chip</code></a></td>
        <td>“this is what I filtered / chose”</td>
        <td>A control that changes the content, not a view of it</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <h3>Do use it when</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>The views are <strong>siblings</strong> — same object, different facets,
        and switching is cheap (no navigation, no data loss).</li>
    <li>There are <strong>2–4</strong> of them; that is what fits as equal cells
        on a phone without truncating labels.</li>
    <li>More than four labels exist but the set is <strong>ordered or
        labelled</strong> — use <code>.fl-tabs--scroll</code>, not a drawer.</li>
    <li>The tab needs an <strong>icon plus a label</strong> for fast recognition
        (the 72px stacked bar), or the set is icon-only and space is tight.</li>
  </ul>
  <h3>Don't use it when</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>The views are <strong>sequential steps</strong> — that is a stepper, and
        “step 2 of 4” is not a tab.</li>
    <li>Switching <strong>navigates</strong> (new URL, new screen) — use links;
        tabs are client-side views, and a bookmark cannot restore a radio
        choice.</li>
    <li>There is <strong>one</strong> tab, or the “views” are really sections of
        one long document — use headings.</li>
    <li>You want tabs to <strong>swipe</strong> — deliberately not shipped (see
        Behavior). Pin that content in place or use a carousel, not tabs.</li>
  </ul>
</section>

<section class="docs-section">
  <h2 id="d-anatomy">Anatomy</h2>
  <div class="fl-tabs">
    <div class="fl-tabs__bar" role="radiogroup" aria-label="Example tabs">
      <label class="fl-tabs__item">
        <input class="fl-tabs__input" type="radio" name="anatomy" checked>
        <span class="fl-tabs__label">Selected</span>
      </label>
      <label class="fl-tabs__item">
        <input class="fl-tabs__input" type="radio" name="anatomy">
        <span class="fl-tabs__label">Inactive</span>
      </label>
      <label class="fl-tabs__item">
        <input class="fl-tabs__input" type="radio" name="anatomy" disabled>
        <span class="fl-tabs__label">Disabled</span>
      </label>
    </div>
    <div class="fl-tabs__panels">
      <div class="fl-tabs__panel">
        <p style="padding-top:16px; color:var(--fl-text-secondary);">The
           selected panel is the only one in the layout and the accessibility
           tree.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="fl-tabs__panel">
        <p style="padding-top:16px;">Inactive panel.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="fl-tabs__panel">
        <p style="padding-top:16px;">Disabled panel.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Bar</strong> — 48px, one row, 1px divider underneath
        (<code>--fl-divider</code>). <code>--fl-tabs-h</code> changes the
        height; the 72px icon bar sets its own.</li>
    <li><strong>Tab cell</strong> — a <code>&lt;label&gt;</code> wrapping the
        radio. Equal shares of the bar on phones; auto-width and centered from
        905px.</li>
    <li><strong>Indicator</strong> — a 2px bottom border in
        <code>--fl-accent</code>, on the selected cell only. It sits on the
        bar's divider line, not below it.</li>
    <li><strong>Label</strong> — 14px, medium weight, all caps
        (<code>--fl-tabs-transform</code>), one line, ellipsized if the cell
        is too narrow.</li>
    <li><strong>Icon</strong> — optional, 24px, stacked above the label.
        <code>.fl-tabs--icons</code> makes the bar 72px.</li>
    <li><strong>Panels</strong> — one <code>.fl-tabs__panel</code> per tab, in
        the same order, inside <code>.fl-tabs__panels</code>.</li>
  </ol>
  {{ callout({
    variant: "warn",
    title: "Order is the contract",
    text: "The k-th tab shows the k-th panel — that is how the mapping stays generic CSS with no per-instance IDs and no script. Reorder the tabs and you must reorder the panels; the cap is 8 tabs."
  }) }}
</section>

<section class="docs-section">
  <h2 id="d-behavior">Behavior</h2>
  <h3>State lives in the radio</h3>
  <p>Each cell wraps a real <code>&lt;input type="radio"&gt;</code> clipped to
     1px, like <a href="chips.html#d-behavior">chips</a>. Clicking the cell,
     the label or the indicator checks that radio; the browser unchecks its
     siblings because they share a <code>name</code>. The bar and the panel
     change are pure CSS — <strong>zero JavaScript, zero resizing</strong>.</p>
  <h3>Keyboard is native</h3>
  <p><kbd>Tab</kbd> reaches the selected radio (only one radio per group is
     tabbable). <kbd>←</kbd>/<kbd>→</kbd> move within the group and select as
     they go, exactly like a radio group everywhere else on the platform.</p>
  <h3>Fixed, icon, scrollable</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Fixed</strong> (default) — cells share the bar equally. Use it
        for ≤4 short labels.</li>
    <li><strong>Icon</strong> — <code>.fl-tabs--icons</code> stacks a 24px
        icon above the label in a 72px bar. Icons are author content; the
        <code>.fl-icon</code> slot is a placeholder, not an icon set.</li>
    <li><strong>Scrollable</strong> — <code>.fl-tabs--scroll</code> keeps one
        row, gives each cell a 90px floor and lets the bar scroll natively
        with <code>scroll-snap</code>. The clipped tab is the affordance; no
        arrows, no script.</li>
  </ul>
  <h3>Phone, tablet, laptop</h3>
  <p>Up to 905px the bar fills its container: equal cells, which is the M2
     fixed-tab look and the right one for a phone. From 905px the cells become
     auto-width and the group centers — M2's clustered tabs, and the reason a
     tab bar on a laptop does not stretch four words across 1200px. A
     scrollable set keeps starting at its first tab at every width.</p>
  <h3>Deliberately not shipped</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Swipe between panels.</strong> M2 itself warns that tabs
        conflict with horizontally swipeable content, and a swipe that changes
        the view also fights the browser's back gesture. The bar is the
        control.</li>
    <li><strong>Dragging the strip</strong> to scroll, and the scrolling
        indicator choreography — native scrolling covers it.</li>
    <li><strong>Two-line labels, label resizing, mixed icon/no-icon sets</strong>
        — M2 marks all three as don'ts.</li>
    <li><strong>Nested tab bars, tabs below tabs, tabs attached to the bottom
        navigation.</strong></li>
  </ul>
  <h3>One gap worth knowing</h3>
  <p>CSS cannot scroll a bar to a tab that gets selected from outside the bar.
     If your code checks a radio that is scrolled out of view (restoring saved
     state, deep-linking a view), also set
     <code>bar.scrollLeft = item.offsetLeft</code> — or call
     <code>scrollIntoView({ inline: "nearest", block: "nearest" })</code>.</p>
</section>

<section class="docs-section">
  <h2 id="d-accessibility">Accessibility</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>It announces as a radio group, not a tablist.</strong> The
        control is the native radio, so the platform reports “Activity, radio
        button, 2 of 3, selected” and arrow keys work for free. Flint does not
        put <code>role="tab"</code> on a label that cannot take focus — that
        would be a lie the keyboard cannot back up.</li>
    <li><strong>Known gap: no WAI-ARIA tablist semantics.</strong> There is no
        <code>aria-selected</code>, no <code>aria-controls</code>, no
        <kbd>Home</kbd>/<kbd>End</kbd>. To help screen-reader users, give each
        panel a heading, and put the group's purpose in
        <code>aria-label</code> (or a <code>fieldset</code>/<code>legend</code>).
        The alternative — JS toggling ARIA and roving tabindex — is the one
        thing this component exists to avoid.</li>
    <li><strong>Hidden panels leave the page.</strong> Inactive panels are
        <code>display:none</code>: not reachable by <kbd>Tab</kbd>, not read
        aloud, and not in the accessibility tree. That is the correct
        behavior for a view switcher.</li>
    <li><strong>Focus ring on the cell.</strong> The ring is drawn on the
        <code>label</code> (inside it, so a scrolling bar cannot clip it) and
        follows <code>:focus-visible</code> — pointer clicks do not show it.</li>
    <li><strong>Touch targets.</strong> Every cell is at least 48px tall and
        as wide as its share of the bar; the indicator is decoration, the whole
        cell is the target.</li>
    <li><strong>Works without JS — and degrades without <code>:has()</code>.</strong>
        Switching is CSS plus native radios. On a pre-2023 engine without
        <code>:has()</code>, no tab can be singled out, so
        <em>every</em> panel is shown stacked and the bar stays a working radio
        group: content is never silently lost.</li>
  </ul>
</section>
{% endset %}

